bcc-cpp: -H writes floating constants in hexadecimal, for the XENIX
bcc-cc1, which has no atof (patch 13); cc passes it.

Each decimal floating constant becomes the hexadecimal one with the same
value, 0x1.hhhhhhhhhhhhhp+d (0x0.hhhhhhhhhhhhhp-1022 for zero and the
denormals; 0x1.0000000000000p1024 for one too large, which is infinity).
The value comes from strtod, which in this project's C library rounds
correctly, as the host's does, so the compiler gets the same double either
way. A suffix (f, L) stays after the new text. Text that strtod does not
take whole (1e, say) is left for the compiler to report.

The tokeniser now also sees .5, 0.5 and 017e2 as floating constants: it
took .5 as a dot and a number, and a leading 0 as the start of an octal
number that ended at the dot or the e. Without -H its output is the same
as before.

